7.4  Virtual machines and hypervisors

TL;DR
  - A virtual machine runs a whole operating system, kernel included, on virtual
    hardware. The hypervisor shares the real CPUs, RAM and devices between
    machines.
  - CPUs help directly (Intel VT-x, AMD-V, ARM's EL2): guest code runs at full
    speed, and only some operations go to the hypervisor.
  - On Linux, KVM turns the kernel into a hypervisor; QEMU provides the virtual
    devices around it.

      systemd-detect-virt          # none, kvm, vmware, docker…
      grep -cw -E 'vmx|svm' /proc/cpuinfo   # 0: no hardware virtualization
      ls -l /dev/kvm               # exists: this machine can run KVM guests

On your machine
  Here      no container or virtual machine detected
  Isolated  none: the host's namespaces (pid, user, uts, ipc, cgroup, time)
  Can run   VMs with KVM (/dev/kvm) · containers with Docker
  Overlays  1 overlay mount: container layers, usually

  Arch      x86_64, made by Intel
  Level     x86-64-v4: programs built for this level or below run here
  Has       SSE 4.2, AVX2 (256-bit vectors), AVX-512, AES instructions, SHA
            instructions, virtualization (Intel VT-x)
  Flags     145 in all, in /proc/cpuinfo

Pitfalls
  - No /dev/kvm: virtualization is off in the firmware settings, or you're
    already in a VM without nested virtualization.
  - Emulated disks and network cards are slow; virtio devices, designed for VMs,
    are what to pick.
  - A guest's clock and CPU numbers are what the hypervisor shows: CPU "steal"
    time in top (st) is time the host gave to others.
  - WSL 2, Docker Desktop and OrbStack are Linux virtual machines; files shared
    with the host cross a slower boundary.

Deep dive

  ▾ How does a CPU run a guest kernel safely?

    Hardware virtualization adds a mode below the guest kernel. The guest runs
    its own kernel code natively, but chosen events (touching certain registers,
    some instructions, device access) exit to the hypervisor, which handles them
    and resumes the guest. A second level of page tables translates guest memory
    to host memory in hardware, so guest page tables work untouched.

  ▾ Type 1 or type 2?

    Type 1 hypervisors run on the bare machine (Xen, VMware ESXi, Hyper-V); type
    2 run as programs on an operating system (VirtualBox). KVM blurs it: a
    module of an ordinary Linux kernel, with each VM an ordinary QEMU process
    you can see in top.

Related
  Commands  systemd-detect-virt, virsh, lscpu
  Paths     /dev/kvm
  See also  idk 7.1 What a container really is
  See also  idk 2.1 What a CPU does: instructions and architectures
